---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
import { tally } from '../lib/rank';
const all = await getCollection('disclosures', ({ data }) => !data.draft);
const t = tally(all);
---
<Base title="About" description="How dutchypoo finds and reports vulnerabilities in open-source C and C++.">
  <section class="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">// about</span>
    <h1>I look for the code nobody is fuzzing.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Independent security researcher working on <strong>memory-safety
      vulnerabilities in open-source C and C++</strong>. {t.cves} CVEs, {t.advisories} GitHub
      Security Advisories, {t.total} public disclosures across {t.projects} projects.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="about">
    <div class="shead"><h2><span class="caret">&#9656;</span>method</h2></div>
    <p>Mature projects with existing fuzzing infrastructure are usually described as picked
      clean. In practice the opposite is true in specific, findable places. A project's
      OSS-Fuzz build configuration disables channels. Its harnesses write input to a temp
      file with no extension, so every filter selected by file extension is unreachable.
      Its parsers are hammered but the consumers downstream of them are never initialised.
      None of that is hidden. It is written down in the build files.</p>
    <p>So the first thing I do on a new target is read the fuzzing configuration, not the
      source. That tells me which code ships to users but has never been executed under a
      sanitizer. Then I build a harness that reaches it. Two of the findings on this site
      came directly out of that step, in code that had been in an OSS-Fuzz project for
      years.</p>

    <div class="shead"><h2><span class="caret">&#9656;</span>how I report</h2></div>
    <p>Private disclosure first wherever the project has a process for it, public issue
      where the maintainer's policy says to do that instead. I include a minimised
      reproducer, the root cause with file and line numbers, and a patch when I can write
      one. Two of these were merged upstream verbatim, comments included.</p>
    <p>I also say what I did not verify. Several reports here carry an explicit open
      question or a limitation I could not resolve. That is deliberate. A report that
      overstates one thing makes a maintainer re-check everything else in it, and I would
      rather be trusted than impressive.</p>

    <div class="shead"><h2><span class="caret">&#9656;</span>tooling</h2></div>
    <p><strong>Fuzzing</strong> libFuzzer, AddressSanitizer, UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer,
      custom in-tree and standalone harnesses, hand-built corpora and dictionaries derived
      from the format spec rather than scraped.<br />
      <strong>Triage</strong> gdb, llvm-symbolizer, delta minimisation, targeted source
      instrumentation when a backtrace is not enough.<br />
      <strong>Web</strong> Burp Suite, ffuf, nmap, Wireshark.<br />
      <strong>Environment</strong> Debian, Kali, Docker, bash, git.</p>

    <div class="shead"><h2><span class="caret">&#9656;</span>contact</h2></div>
    <p>Open to security research and application security work, remote or Austin, Texas.
      Reports, questions and corrections all welcome.</p>
    <div class="contacts">
      <a href={SITE.github}>github.com/dutchypoo &#8599;</a>
      <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
      <a href="/disclosures">disclosures</a>
      <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">security.txt</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
